 (success rc msg)←ExecUnderTrap expr;qdmx;valence;hasResult;cmd
⍝ Executes `expr` under a :Trap 0 and returns (1 0 '') in case of success and (0 ⎕en <errorMessage>) otherwise.
⍝ If `expr` is a function that returns a result, that result will be ignored.
⍝ If `expr` is a function that requires a right argument, for the time being ⎕NULL is passed.
⍝ A dyadic function cannot be called at all, and that is a failure like any other. `rc` is then ¯1
⍝ because no ⎕EN applies. It used to be reported as a *success*: the complaint went straight to the
⍝ session with `⍞←` and execution fell through to `success←1` below, so the caller was told all was
⍝ well and carried on as though the function had run.\\
⍝ `msg` is shaped the way `⎕DM` is, a vector of character vectors, because that is what the caller
⍝ gets in every other failing case.
 success←1
 rc←0
 msg←''
 :Trap 0
     hasResult←(10 ⎕ATX expr)∊1 ¯1
     valence←11 ⎕ATX expr
     :If valence=0              ⍝ Niladic
         cmd←(hasResult/'{}'),expr
         ⍎cmd
     :ElseIf valence∊1 ¯2       ⍝ Monadic or ambivalent
         cmd←(hasResult/'{}'),expr,' ⎕NULL'
         ⍎cmd
     :Else                      ⍝ That leaves a dyadic call
         success←0
         rc←¯1
         msg←,⊂'The function ',expr,' is dyadic and can therefore not be called'
     :EndIf
 :Else
     qdmx←⎕DMX
     success←0
     rc←qdmx.EN
     msg←qdmx.DM
 :EndTrap
